From 09dab18e91570044eb142eeb696a7a7a1bf2b6ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: junglivre <junglivre@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 22:41:38 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Read big deduplicated files correctly, fail with EIO
 otherwise

The stream map of a file lists one entry per chunk, about 1400 for
100 MiB. Only the first MAXSMAP (512) entries were looked at for the
start of a read, so a read crossing the end of that window came back
short. The kernel takes a short read as the end of file: files over
about 37 MiB were silently truncated, or zero-filled when the size was
refreshed later, and no error was ever reported.

- read the stream map through a window of MAXSMAP entries that moves
  along with the read, after a dichotomic search for the first entry
- check every chunk against its stream map entry: the entry records
  the first 32 bytes of the SHA-512 of the uncompressed chunk
- check the stream map itself: increasing data ends, chunks of at most
  128K, inside the part, covering the whole read
- bound the decompressor reads to the compressed buffer
- read every data entry of format 0x0103 instead of only the first
- return EIO when any of this fails, and never a short count before
  the end of file
- keep the last checked chunk, most reads begin where the previous one
  ended
---
 src/dedup.c | 996 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----------------------
 1 file changed, 561 insertions(+), 435 deletions(-)

